# Target C: global good-side normal-cone slack and near-Reinhardt carrier sea

**Date:** 2026-10-02\
**Base:** October full actual-diameter / leaf-fan stack\
**Status:** proof-only exact geometric ledger. C-II and Level I remain open.

## 1. Setup

Work with the canonical post-ZF C-II source. Let

\[
B=\{b_1,\ldots,b_q\}
\]

be the loaded force-hull vertices in cyclic order, and let

\[
H=(B,E_H),\qquad h=|E_H|,\qquad d_v=\deg_H(v).
\]

Put

\[
\delta_D:=2\arcsin\frac1{2D}.
\tag{1}
\]

Every actual diameter edge has a distinct projective direction, and the canonical
diameter-gap theorem gives consecutive projective gaps at least \(\delta_D\). Thus

\[
\boxed{
E_{\rm dir}:=\pi-h\delta_D\ge0.
}
\tag{2}
\]

Let \(\theta_v\) be the exterior turning angle, equivalently the width of the
exterior normal cone, at loaded hull vertex \(v\).

Call the loaded hull interval between two consecutive loaded vertices **good** when
it is one direct unit-contact edge. Let

\[
G=\#\{\text{good loaded hull intervals}\},
\qquad
B_{\rm bad}:=q-G.
\tag{3}
\]

For a loaded vertex \(v\), let

\[
g_v\in\{0,1,2\}
\]

be the number of its two adjacent loaded hull intervals that are good. Then

\[
\sum_{v\in B}g_v=2G.
\tag{4}
\]

## 2. Each good side buys half a minimum-gap unit of normal clearance

Fix a loaded vertex \(v\), an incident diameter partner \(k\), and an adjacent good
unit hull neighbor \(c\). Put

\[
n={v-k\over D}.
\]

The diameter condition gives

\[
|c-k|^2\le D^2=|v-k|^2.
\]

Since \(|v-c|=1\),

\[
|c-k|^2
=
D^2+1-2D\,n\cdot(v-c).
\]

Hence

\[
n\cdot(v-c)\ge\frac1{2D}.
\tag{5}
\]

The boundary ray of the normal cone corresponding to the hull side \(vc\) is
perpendicular to \(v-c\). Therefore the angular distance of \(n\) from that boundary
ray is at least

\[
\arcsin\frac1{2D}
=
\frac{\delta_D}{2}.
\tag{6}
\]

Distinct diameter normals incident with \(v\) are separated by at least
\(\delta_D\), because their partners are unit-separated on the radius-\(D\) circle.

Ordering the \(d_v\) incident diameter normals inside the normal cone therefore gives

\[
\boxed{
\theta_v
\ge
\left(d_v-1+\frac{g_v}{2}\right)\delta_D.
}
\tag{7}
\]

This simultaneously contains three cases:

- no adjacent good side: \(\theta_v\ge(d_v-1)\delta_D\);
- one good side: \(\theta_v\ge(d_v-\tfrac12)\delta_D\);
- two good sides: \(\theta_v\ge d_v\delta_D\).

The last inequality is the local packing estimate previously used only in the
Reinhardt/cyclic endpoint; no cyclic or \(d_H\le2\) hypothesis is needed.

## 3. Exact global slack identity

Define the local normal-cone slack

\[
\sigma_v
:=
\theta_v-
\left(d_v-1+\frac{g_v}{2}\right)\delta_D
\ge0.
\tag{8}
\]

The loaded set is the force-hull vertex set, so

\[
\sum_v\theta_v=2\pi.
\tag{9}
\]

Also

\[
\sum_v(d_v-1)=2h-q,
\qquad
\frac12\sum_vg_v=G.
\]

Therefore

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_v\sigma_v
&=
2\pi-\delta_D(2h-q+G)\\
&=
2\pi-\delta_D(2h-B_{\rm bad}).
\end{aligned}
\]

Using (2):

### Theorem 3.1 -- good-side normal-cone ledger

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{v\in B}\sigma_v
=
2E_{\rm dir}+B_{\rm bad}\delta_D.
}
\tag{10}
\]

Every term on both sides is nonnegative.

Thus all failure of local minimum-width diameter-star packing is paid by exactly two
global currencies:

1. projective diameter-direction gap excess;
2. missing direct unit sides on the loaded hull.

No KKT coefficient appears.

## 4. Outer-walk control of the bad-side term

Split the outer positive-contact boundary walk into the \(q\) loaded-to-loaded
subwalks in cyclic order. If the \(i\)-th subwalk has length \(\ell_i\), then

\[
\ell_i\ge1,
\qquad
\sum_i(\ell_i-1)=x_B.
\tag{11}
\]

A loaded hull interval is good exactly when \(\ell_i=1\). Every bad interval has
\(\ell_i-1\ge1\). Hence

\[
\boxed{
B_{\rm bad}\le x_B.
}
\tag{12}
\]

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
\sum_v\sigma_v
\le
2E_{\rm dir}+x_B\delta_D.
}
\tag{13}
\]

## 5. Collapse in the low-detour square-root branch

Assume

\[
x_B=o(D),
\qquad
\nu(H)=O(\sqrt D).
\tag{14}
\]

The near-circle theorem gives

\[
q/D\to\pi.
\]

Woodall gives

\[
d_H=q-h\le\nu=o(D),
\]

so

\[
h/D\to\pi.
\tag{15}
\]

Since

\[
\delta_D=\frac1D+O(D^{-3}),
\]

equation (15) gives

\[
E_{\rm dir}
=
\pi-h\delta_D
=o(1).
\tag{16}
\]

By (12),

\[
B_{\rm bad}\delta_D
\le
x_B\delta_D
=o(1).
\tag{17}
\]

Insert (16)--(17) into Theorem 3.1:

\[
\boxed{
\sum_v\sigma_v=o(1).
}
\tag{18}
\]

This is a global near-equality theorem: the complete loaded hull has only \(o(1)\)
total angular slack beyond the mandatory diameter-star packing and good-side
clearance.

## 6. Internal star-gap excess is also paid by the same ledger

Fix \(v\) with \(d_v\ge2\), and order its incident diameter directions in the
normal cone. Write their consecutive angular gaps as

\[
\gamma_{v,1},\ldots,\gamma_{v,d_v-1},
\qquad
\gamma_{v,j}\ge\delta_D.
\]

Let

\[
X_v
:=
\sum_{j=1}^{d_v-1}(\gamma_{v,j}-\delta_D)
\ge0.
\tag{19}
\]

The proof of (7) decomposes the normal-cone width into the two boundary clearances
plus these internal gaps. Every clearance term beyond the mandatory
\(g_v\delta_D/2\) and every internal gap excess is nonnegative. Therefore

\[
\boxed{0\le X_v\le\sigma_v.}
\tag{20}
\]

Hence under (14),

\[
\boxed{
\sum_vX_v=o(1).
}
\tag{21}
\]

So the internal spacing of all diameter stars is globally almost minimal in total,
not only on average over directions.

## 7. Combine with the balanced square-root carrier sea

The companion carrier theorem on the current research branch proves that, under
(14), there are \(\Omega(\sqrt D)\) **medium carriers** satisfying

\[
a_K\sqrt D
\le
t_v-1
\le
B_*\sqrt D
\tag{22}
\]

for fixed positive constants \(a_K,B_*\), where \(t_v\) is the number of pendant
diameter leaves at \(v\).

In particular each medium carrier has

\[
d_v=\Theta(\sqrt D),
\qquad
d_v\delta_D=\Theta(D^{-1/2}).
\tag{23}
\]

Fix any \(\varepsilon>0\). By (18),

\[
\#\left\{
v\text{ medium}:
\sigma_v>\frac{\varepsilon}{\sqrt D}
\right\}
\le
\frac{\sqrt D}{\varepsilon}\sum_v\sigma_v
=
o(\sqrt D).
\tag{24}
\]

Thus all but \(o(\sqrt D)\) medium carriers satisfy

\[
\boxed{
\sigma_v\le\frac{\varepsilon}{\sqrt D}.
}
\tag{25}
\]

Since \(\varepsilon\) is arbitrary, a full-density subfamily of the medium carriers
has

\[
\boxed{
\sigma_v=o(D^{-1/2}),
\qquad
X_v=o(D^{-1/2}).
}
\tag{26}
\]

For those carriers,

\[
\boxed{
\theta_v
=
d_v\delta_D+o(D^{-1/2}),
}
\tag{27}
\]

because the possible missing boundary-clearance correction
\((1-g_v/2)\delta_D=O(D^{-1})\) is smaller than \(D^{-1/2}\).

If \(\Theta_v\) denotes the angular span from the first to the last incident diameter
direction, then

\[
\Theta_v
=
(d_v-1)\delta_D+X_v,
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
\Theta_v
=
(d_v-1)\delta_D+o(D^{-1/2}).
}
\tag{28}
\]

Relative to the natural medium-star scale \(d_v\delta_D=\Theta(D^{-1/2})\), both
(27) and (28) are asymptotic equalities.

## 8. Interpretation: a near-Reinhardt square-root star sea

The low-detour square-root branch now contains a positive square-root population of
actual diameter carriers with all of the following simultaneous properties:

- degree \(\Theta(\sqrt D)\);
- \(\Theta(\sqrt D)\) pendant leaves in one consecutive hull block;
- star interval width \(\Theta(D^{-1/2})\);
- normal-cone width equal to \(d_v\delta_D\) up to \(o(D^{-1/2})\);
- total internal diameter-gap excess \(o(D^{-1/2})\);
- almost all adjacent leaf-block chords are exact unit contacts.

This is the finite-defect analogue of the exact cyclic Reinhardt relation

\[
\theta_v=d_v\delta_D.
\]

The conclusion does **not** imply a common uniform KKT stress, exact equal gaps, or
membership in the Reinhardt equality class. It does remove macroscopic angular
slack from the carrier sea.

For the October train problem, the remaining split-star witnesses therefore live
among \(\Theta(\sqrt D)\) broad, almost-uniform diameter-star grids rather than
arbitrary direction packets.


## 9. Pendant leaves are a contiguous subblock in star-direction order

The leaf-fan theorem states that, for one nonleaf carrier \(a\), its degree-one
diameter neighbors \(L(a)\) form one consecutive block in loaded-hull cyclic order.
There is a parallel direction-order consequence.

### Lemma 9.1

The diameter rays from \(a\) to the vertices of \(L(a)\) form one consecutive
subblock among all actual diameter rays incident with \(a\).

### Proof

All diameter partners of \(a\) lie on the same circle

\[
\partial B(a,D),
\]

and the entire source lies inside \(B(a,D)\). The full star has angular span at most
\(\pi/3\), so its partner rays have one unambiguous ordinary angular order.

Suppose another diameter partner \(z\notin L(a)\) had angular direction strictly
between two consecutive leaf rays \(ap,aq\) in that order. The points \(p,z,q\) all
lie on the boundary circle of \(B(a,D)\). Tangent lines to that disk support the
source hull at those points, so their cyclic order on the source hull agrees with
their order on this short circle arc. Hence \(z\) lies on the open loaded-hull arc
from \(p\) to \(q\) that does not contain \(a\).

But the leaf-fan hull-order lemma says every loaded vertex on that open arc is again
a degree-one diameter neighbor of \(a\). Thus \(z\in L(a)\), contradiction. QED.

Order the leaf rays in this common hull/direction order:

\[
ap_1,\ldots,ap_{t_a}.
\]

Put

\[
Y_a
:=
\sum_{i=1}^{t_a-1}
\left(
\angle p_iap_{i+1}-\delta_D
\right)
\ge0.
\tag{29}
\]

By Lemma 9.1 these are internal consecutive gaps of the full star, so

\[
\boxed{0\le Y_a\le X_a\le\sigma_a.}
\tag{30}
\]

Therefore every near-equality medium carrier from Section 7 has pendant-block span

\[
\boxed{
\angle p_1ap_{t_a}
=
(t_a-1)\delta_D+o(D^{-1/2}).
}
\tag{31}
\]

## 10. Typical medium blocks are almost entirely exact unit-chord fans

Let \(b_a\) be the number of internal pendant-block adjacencies
\(p_ip_{i+1}\) that are not good direct unit hull contacts.

These bad adjacencies are distinct bad loaded hull intervals. Hence

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{a\in\mathcal C}b_a\le B_{\rm bad}\le x_B=o(D).
}
\tag{32}
\]

The balanced-carrier theorem gives \(M_D=\Omega(\sqrt D)\) medium carriers.
Since the total in (32) is \(o(D)\), there is a subfamily of

\[
M_D-o(\sqrt D)
\]

medium carriers for which

\[
\boxed{b_a=o(\sqrt D).}
\tag{33}
\]

One explicit Markov choice is the following. If \(x_B>0\), set

\[
\eta_D:=\sqrt{x_B/D}=o(1).
\]

The number of carriers with \(b_a>\eta_D\sqrt D\) is at most

\[
\frac{x_B}{\eta_D\sqrt D}
=
\sqrt{x_B}
=
o(\sqrt D).
\]

When \(x_B=0\) the statement is immediate.

Intersect this subfamily with the full-density near-equality subfamily from
Section 7. For every retained medium carrier,

\[
t_a=\Theta(\sqrt D),
\qquad
b_a=o(\sqrt D),
\qquad
Y_a=o(D^{-1/2}).
\tag{34}
\]

Every good adjacency has exact chord length one and exact carrier-angle
\(\delta_D\). Thus:

\[
\boxed{
\text{for a positive }\sqrt D\text{-population of carriers, }
(1-o(1))t_a
\text{ consecutive leaf adjacencies are exact }(D,D,1)\text{ fan contacts.}
}
\tag{35}
\]

The non-good adjacencies occupy a vanishing fraction of each retained block, and the
entire block's angular span differs from the minimum
\((t_a-1)\delta_D\) by only \(o(D^{-1/2})\).

In particular every retained block contains a consecutive exact good-fan run whose
length tends to infinity. Indeed \(b_a+1=o(\sqrt D)+1\) good runs partition
\(\Theta(\sqrt D)\) leaf adjacencies, so the largest run has length

\[
\frac{\Theta(\sqrt D)}{b_a+1}\longrightarrow\infty.
\tag{36}
\]

This does not force an \(\Omega(\sqrt D)\) run unless the stronger
\(x_B=O(\sqrt D)\) regime is assumed. It does force the remaining low-detour hard
branch to contain \(\Omega(\sqrt D)\) almost-minimal carrier arcs, each with a
vanishing fraction of nonunit internal hull transitions.


**Disposition:** proof-only exact ledger and asymptotic reduction. No computation,
numerical search, verifier, or CI output is used.

